-- CISSP Domain 1 expansion, part D of 4: from-scratch coverage for the three
-- previously-uncovered objectives 1.11 (threat modeling: STRIDE/PASTA/attack
-- trees/DREAD/threat actor modeling), 1.12 (supply chain risk management:
-- vendor risk assessment/SLAs/ongoing monitoring/hardware & software supply
-- chain integrity), and 1.13 (security awareness, education, and training
-- programs). Adds flashcards ci-f-500..535, ci-a-190..198, ci-y-110..118
-- and questions ci-q-500..544.

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 1.11 threat modeling: core
('ci-f-500','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What is an attack tree in threat modeling?$$,$$A diagram that models an attacker's goal as a root node, with branching child nodes representing alternative and combined ways (AND/OR) to achieve that goal, letting analysts systematically enumerate attack paths and prioritize defenses against the most feasible ones.$$),
('ci-f-501','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What do the five DREAD scoring criteria measure?$$,$$Damage (how bad is the impact), Reproducibility (how easily can the attack be repeated), Exploitability (how much effort/skill is required), Affected users (how many are impacted), and Discoverability (how easily can the vulnerability be found) - each scored numerically and combined to rank threats by relative severity.$$),
('ci-f-502','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What is a trust boundary in a data flow diagram (DFD), and why does it matter for threat modeling?$$,$$A trust boundary marks where data crosses between zones of differing privilege or trust, such as the internet and an internal network. Threat modelers focus scrutiny on these crossing points because attackers most often exploit the transition where trust assumptions change.$$),
('ci-f-503','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What are the three core approaches to threat modeling: attacker-centric, asset-centric, and software-centric?$$,$$Attacker-centric starts from adversary profiles and their likely goals/techniques. Asset-centric starts from what needs protecting and works outward to threats against it. Software/system-centric (e.g., STRIDE applied to a DFD) starts from the system's architecture and enumerates threats against each component and data flow.$$),
('ci-f-504','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What is the overall goal of the PASTA threat modeling methodology?$$,$$PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis) is a seven-stage, risk-centric methodology that ties technical threats to business objectives and attacker motivation, producing a risk-ranked view of threats rather than just a category list.$$),
('ci-f-505','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What is the difference between attack surface and attack vector?$$,$$The attack surface is the complete set of points where an unauthorized user could try to enter or extract data - every exposed interface, port, and input. An attack vector is the specific path or technique used for a single attack, such as a phishing email or an unpatched service.$$),
('ci-f-506','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$How do capability, motivation, and opportunity combine when modeling a threat actor?$$,$$Capability is the actor's skill and resources; motivation is why they would target you (financial, ideological, espionage); opportunity is whether an exploitable weakness and access exist. All three must be present for a threat actor to pose a realistic risk - removing any one reduces the threat.$$),
('ci-f-507','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What are Trike and VAST, and how do they differ from STRIDE?$$,$$Trike is a risk-based methodology that generates threats from an explicit acceptable-risk model tied to stakeholder-approved permissions. VAST (Visual, Agile, Simple Threat modeling) is designed to scale across large agile organizations by integrating threat modeling into every sprint. Both aim for broader organizational scale than STRIDE's per-element, developer-focused checklist approach.$$),
('ci-f-508','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$Why do threat modelers typically build a Data Flow Diagram (DFD) before applying STRIDE?$$,$$STRIDE is applied element-by-element - to processes, data stores, data flows, and external entities - so a DFD gives the structured map of those elements and their trust boundaries that STRIDE needs in order to systematically ask which threat categories apply to each one.$$),
('ci-f-509','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$How does a nation-state APT actor typically differ from a hacktivist in threat modeling terms?$$,$$A nation-state advanced persistent threat (APT) is well-funded, highly skilled, and motivated by long-term espionage or strategic advantage, favoring stealth and persistence. A hacktivist is typically less resourced, motivated by ideology, and favors visible, disruptive action (defacement, DDoS, leaks) over quiet persistence.$$),
('ci-f-510','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$What is a key weakness of DREAD scoring compared to STRIDE categorization?$$,$$DREAD's five criteria are scored subjectively by whoever performs the assessment, so different reviewers can rate the same threat very differently, undermining consistency; STRIDE avoids this by only categorizing threat type rather than assigning a numeric severity score.$$),
('ci-f-511','cissp','core',1,'1.11',$$At what point in the SDLC is threat modeling most valuable, and why?$$,$$During design, before code is written - threats identified against the architecture can be addressed through design changes at low cost, whereas the same flaw found after deployment requires far more expensive rework, patching, and possibly incident response.$$),
-- 1.12 supply chain risk management: core
('ci-f-512','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)?$$,$$A formal, machine-readable inventory of every component, library, and dependency in a piece of software, including versions and origins - used to quickly identify whether a newly disclosed vulnerability affects a product without manually auditing the codebase.$$),
('ci-f-513','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is a right-to-audit clause, and why is it included in vendor contracts?$$,$$A contractual provision giving the customer (or its designated auditor) the right to inspect a vendor's security controls, records, or facilities during the relationship - it turns ongoing assurance from a courtesy the vendor might grant into an enforceable contractual obligation.$$),
('ci-f-514','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is fourth-party risk?$$,$$Risk introduced by your vendor's own subcontractors and suppliers (the vendor's vendors), which you typically cannot assess or contract with directly - it must be managed indirectly by requiring your vendor to flow down security obligations to its own supply chain.$$),
('ci-f-515','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What are the typical components of a Service Level Agreement (SLA)?$$,$$Measurable performance commitments such as uptime/availability percentage, response and resolution time targets, defined penalties or service credits for missed targets, and often explicit security obligations - giving both parties an objective basis for measuring whether the vendor is meeting expectations.$$),
('ci-f-516','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is vendor risk tiering, and what typically determines a vendor's tier?$$,$$A method of sorting vendors into risk categories (e.g., critical, high, moderate, low) based on factors like the sensitivity of data they access, their access to critical systems, and how difficult they would be to replace - so assessment depth and monitoring frequency can be matched to actual risk rather than applied uniformly to every vendor.$$),
('ci-f-517','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is source code escrow, and when is it used?$$,$$An arrangement where a vendor's source code is deposited with a neutral third party and released to the customer under defined trigger conditions, such as the vendor going out of business or abandoning the product - it protects customers who depend on software they cannot otherwise maintain themselves.$$),
('ci-f-518','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is a hardware supply chain integrity concern, such as a hardware implant or counterfeit component?$$,$$The risk that physical components - chips, boards, or entire devices - are tampered with, substituted with counterfeits, or have malicious circuitry added at some point between design and delivery, potentially creating a backdoor or reliability failure that software-level controls cannot detect or remove.$$),
('ci-f-519','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is code signing, and how does it protect the software supply chain?$$,$$A cryptographic signature applied by the software publisher that lets a recipient verify both the authenticity of the source and that the code has not been altered since signing - it defends against tampered updates and impersonated publishers being installed as trusted software.$$),
('ci-f-520','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is a dependency confusion attack?$$,$$An attack where a malicious package is published to a public repository using the same name as an internal, private package; if a build system is misconfigured to prefer the public registry, it pulls and executes the attacker's malicious package instead of the legitimate internal one.$$),
('ci-f-521','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What is the purpose of NIST SP 800-161 in supply chain risk management?$$,$$It provides US federal guidance on identifying, assessing, and mitigating cybersecurity risks throughout the information and communications technology supply chain, covering practices like vendor vetting, component provenance, and ongoing monitoring, and is widely referenced beyond government as a supply-chain risk management framework.$$),
('ci-f-522','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$Why does vendor offboarding carry security risk that requires deliberate management?$$,$$When a vendor relationship ends, access credentials, data copies, integrations, and physical access must all be formally revoked and data returned or destroyed - if offboarding is treated as an afterthought, a former vendor can retain live access or unreturned data long after the business relationship is over.$$),
('ci-f-523','cissp','core',1,'1.12',$$What are minimum security requirements clauses in vendor contracts?$$,$$Contract language that obligates a vendor to maintain specific, named security controls (such as encryption standards, breach notification timelines, or a required certification) as a condition of the agreement, turning expected security practices into an enforceable baseline rather than an informal expectation.$$),
-- 1.13 security awareness, education, and training: core
('ci-f-524','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What are the three stages of the NIST SP 800-50 awareness-training-education continuum?$$,$$Awareness (what to do and what not to do - short, broad, repeated reminders), Training (how to do it - skill-building for a specific role or task), and Education (why - deeper, often formal study that produces the judgment to handle situations the training didn't explicitly cover). Each stage builds toward greater depth and a narrower audience.$$),
('ci-f-525','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What is role-based security training?$$,$$Training content tailored to what a specific job function actually needs to know, rather than one generic course for everyone - for example, developers receive secure coding training, system administrators receive privileged-access hygiene training, and general staff receive phishing and social engineering awareness.$$),
('ci-f-526','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$Why is course-completion rate a weak metric for training program effectiveness on its own?$$,$$It only shows that someone clicked through the material, not that they understood it, retained it, or will change their behavior when it matters - a program can show 100% completion and still fail to reduce real-world incidents if the training didn't actually change behavior.$$),
('ci-f-527','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What is 'report rate' in a phishing simulation program, and why does it matter?$$,$$The percentage of employees who correctly identify and report a simulated phishing email (e.g., via a report-phishing button) rather than simply not clicking it. A rising report rate shows employees are actively engaging and helping defend the organization, which is a stronger sign of a healthy security culture than a falling click rate alone.$$),
('ci-f-528','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$Why should security awareness programs avoid a punitive approach toward employees who fail phishing simulations?$$,$$Punishing failures discourages employees from reporting real mistakes or suspicious activity out of fear, which is the opposite of what the program needs - a program built on positive reinforcement and coaching encourages people to self-report and stay engaged, producing better long-term behavior change than shaming does.$$),
('ci-f-529','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$When should new-hire security awareness training occur?$$,$$As part of onboarding, before or immediately upon granting system access - waiting until a later scheduled annual training leaves new employees, who are often specifically targeted by attackers as unfamiliar with company norms, unprepared during their most vulnerable early period.$$),
('ci-f-530','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What distinct focus should executive and board-level security training have compared to general staff training?$$,$$Executives and board members are high-value targets for whaling (targeted spear-phishing) and business email compromise because of their financial authority and access to sensitive decisions, so their training emphasizes recognizing highly targeted, well-researched social engineering rather than the generic mass-phishing content used for general staff.$$),
('ci-f-531','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What is security culture, and how does it differ from compliance-driven training?$$,$$Security culture is the shared, everyday set of attitudes and habits where employees genuinely value and practice good security behavior because they see it as part of doing their job well. Compliance-driven training only aims to satisfy a regulatory checkbox (e.g., an annual course completion requirement) and can leave behavior unchanged even when the requirement is technically met.$$),
('ci-f-532','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$Why should simulated phishing campaign difficulty increase gradually over time?$$,$$Starting with obvious lures and gradually introducing more sophisticated, realistic ones keeps the program calibrated to the organization's actual improving baseline - if difficulty never increases, click rates naturally drop over time without meaningfully testing or improving real-world resilience against convincing attacks.$$),
('ci-f-533','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What are the typical phases of a security awareness and training program lifecycle?$$,$$Design (assess needs and define goals/audiences), Develop (build the actual content and materials), Implement (deliver the training), and Evaluate/Maintain (measure effectiveness and update content) - the cycle repeats as threats, roles, and the workforce change.$$),
('ci-f-534','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$What is a Learning Management System (LMS) used for in a security training program?$$,$$A platform for assigning, delivering, and tracking training modules and quizzes to employees, recording completion status and scores so the program can report on participation and, when paired with behavioral metrics, evaluate effectiveness.$$),
('ci-f-535','cissp','core',1,'1.13',$$Why is measuring behavior change a better long-term indicator of training effectiveness than a knowledge quiz score alone?$$,$$A quiz measures short-term recall of facts, which can fade quickly and doesn't guarantee someone will apply it under pressure. Behavior change - like a sustained drop in real incident reports tied to human error, or a rising phishing report rate over months - shows the training actually altered what people do, which is the outcome the program exists to produce.$$),
-- acronyms
('ci-a-190','cissp','acronym',1,'1.11',$$STRIDE$$,$$Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege - a threat categorization model applied per element of a system diagram to identify threat types.$$),
('ci-a-191','cissp','acronym',1,'1.11',$$PASTA$$,$$Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis - a seven-stage, risk-centric threat modeling methodology that ties technical threats to business impact and attacker motivation.$$),
('ci-a-192','cissp','acronym',1,'1.11',$$DREAD$$,$$Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, Discoverability - a five-criteria scoring model used to rank the relative severity of identified threats.$$),
('ci-a-193','cissp','acronym',1,'1.12',$$SBOM$$,$$Software Bill of Materials - an inventory of a software product's components and dependencies, used to quickly assess exposure when a new vulnerability is disclosed.$$),
('ci-a-194','cissp','acronym',1,'1.12',$$SLA$$,$$Service Level Agreement - a contractual commitment defining measurable performance targets (uptime, response time) and the penalties for missing them.$$),
('ci-a-195','cissp','acronym',1,'1.12',$$TPRM$$,$$Third-Party Risk Management - the ongoing program of assessing, contracting for, and monitoring the security risk introduced by vendors and other external parties throughout the relationship, not just at onboarding.$$),
('ci-a-196','cissp','acronym',1,'1.13',$$SETA$$,$$Security Education, Training, and Awareness - the umbrella term for an organization's program combining broad awareness, role-specific training, and deeper education to build workforce security behavior.$$),
('ci-a-197','cissp','acronym',1,'1.13',$$CBT$$,$$Computer-Based Training - self-paced, interactive training modules delivered digitally, commonly used to scale security awareness content across a large workforce.$$),
('ci-a-198','cissp','acronym',1,'1.13',$$LMS$$,$$Learning Management System - the platform used to assign, deliver, and track completion of training modules across an organization.$$),
-- feynman
('ci-y-110','cissp','feynman',1,'1.11',$$Explain the difference between STRIDE and PASTA to someone new to threat modeling.$$,$$Model answer: Think of STRIDE as a checklist and PASTA as an investigation. STRIDE hands you six labeled buckets - Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege - and for every piece of your system's diagram you ask "could this happen here?" It's fast, developer-friendly, and great for catching technical threats early. PASTA instead walks through seven stages that start from business objectives, build out the technical scope, simulate how a real attacker would move through it, and end by scoring identified threats against actual business impact. STRIDE tells you what kinds of things could go wrong technically; PASTA tells you which of those things actually matter to the business and how an attacker would really pull them off.$$),
('ci-y-111','cissp','feynman',1,'1.11',$$Explain how an attack tree models an attacker's path to a goal.$$,$$Model answer: Draw the attacker's ultimate objective as the root of an upside-down tree - say, "steal the customer database." Underneath, branch into the different ways that goal could be reached: exploit a web app vulnerability, bribe an insider, or steal a backup tape. Each of those branches can split further into concrete sub-steps, and you mark whether reaching a parent node requires ALL its children (AND, meaning every sub-step is necessary) or ANY ONE of them (OR, meaning a single path suffices). Reading the tree bottom-up shows every viable path to the root goal, so defenders can see which single control would block the most branches and prioritize accordingly.$$),
('ci-y-112','cissp','feynman',1,'1.11',$$Explain why modeling a threat actor's capability, motivation, and opportunity together matters more than looking at any one alone.$$,$$Model answer: A skilled nation-state actor (capability) has no reason to attack a hobbyist's blog (no motivation) - so capability alone doesn't create risk. Conversely, someone highly motivated to harm you but with no technical skill and no exploitable weakness to reach you (no opportunity) also poses little practical danger. Real risk emerges only where all three overlap: someone who can, wants to, and is able to reach you. That's why threat actor modeling asks all three questions together instead of just cataloging who might be "dangerous" in the abstract - it turns a vague worry into a prioritized, realistic risk picture.$$),
('ci-y-113','cissp','feynman',1,'1.12',$$Explain why hardware supply chain integrity is harder to verify than software integrity.$$,$$Model answer: Software has mature tools for this - you can hash a file, check a digital signature, or scan source code, and the check is deterministic and repeatable. Hardware doesn't offer that same handle. A malicious modification can be a few extra transistors added to a chip during fabrication, invisible to the naked eye and often invisible to functional testing, because the device still performs its normal job while secretly doing something extra. Verifying hardware really means trusting the chain of custody - vetted fabrication facilities, tamper-evident packaging, and provenance tracking - because you usually cannot open the finished chip and inspect it the way you can read source code.$$),
('ci-y-114','cissp','feynman',1,'1.12',$$Explain the concept of fourth-party risk with an example.$$,$$Model answer: Your company contracts a payroll vendor (your third party). That payroll vendor, in turn, uses a cloud storage provider to hold the data it processes for you (their vendor, your fourth party). You never signed a contract with the cloud provider and can't audit it directly, yet if that provider is breached, your employee data can still leak. Fourth-party risk is exactly that: exposure from your vendor's own supply chain, which you can only manage indirectly - by requiring your vendor's contract to flow down security obligations to whoever it relies on.$$),
('ci-y-115','cissp','feynman',1,'1.12',$$Explain why an SLA alone doesn't guarantee vendor security.$$,$$Model answer: An SLA is a promise about measurable performance - uptime, response times, maybe a breach-notification deadline - backed by financial penalties if missed. But penalties are paid after the fact; they compensate for a failure, they don't prevent it. A vendor can meet every uptime number in its SLA while still running outdated software, skipping patches, or storing your data insecurely, because those things were never written into the agreement as specific, auditable obligations. That's why security programs pair SLAs with explicit minimum security requirements and a right-to-audit clause - the SLA covers performance, but security needs its own enforceable commitments and a way to verify them.$$),
('ci-y-116','cissp','feynman',1,'1.13',$$Explain the NIST SP 800-50 awareness/training/education continuum using a driving analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Awareness is like a road sign reminding every driver "buckle up" - brief, repeated, aimed at everyone, and it doesn't teach a skill, it just prompts a habit. Training is like a driving lesson focused on a specific skill, such as how to parallel park - it builds a concrete, applied ability for people who actually need it. Education is like the deeper study behind traffic engineering itself - understanding why certain intersections are dangerous, so you can reason through situations no single lesson covered. Security awareness reminds everyone of the basics, role-based training builds specific job skills like secure coding, and security education (e.g., a degree or certification) builds the deeper judgment to handle novel situations.$$),
('ci-y-117','cissp','feynman',1,'1.13',$$Explain why click rate alone can be a misleading metric for a phishing simulation program.$$,$$Model answer: A falling click rate looks like success, but it can be misleading two ways. First, employees can learn to recognize your specific simulated templates rather than phishing in general, so the number improves without real resilience improving. Second, click rate says nothing about whether people who avoided clicking also reported the email - silently ignoring a suspicious message leaves it sitting in other inboxes, unreported, while actively reporting it lets security block it organization-wide. That's why mature programs track report rate and vary/escalate simulation difficulty alongside click rate, instead of treating one shrinking number as proof the whole program is working.$$),
('ci-y-118','cissp','feynman',1,'1.13',$$Explain why role-based training matters more than a single generic security course for everyone.$$,$$Model answer: A generic course has to be shallow enough for the least technical audience, so it teaches everyone the same basics - like not clicking suspicious links - regardless of their actual risk exposure. But a developer's biggest risk is writing an injection-vulnerable query, not clicking a link, and a system administrator's biggest risk is privileged-credential misuse, not phishing. If everyone gets the same generic course, the people with the highest-risk jobs never get taught the specific skill that actually protects the organization from their particular failure mode. Role-based training instead matches content depth and topic to what each job actually touches, so the training budget goes where the real risk is.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 1.11 threat modeling (15)
('ci-q-500','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',1,$q$What do the five criteria in DREAD threat scoring stand for?$q$,$q$["Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, Discoverability","Detection, Response, Evidence, Attribution, Disclosure","Data, Risk, Exposure, Access, Defense","Denial, Repudiation, Interception, Disclosure, Escalation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DREAD scores a threat by rating its Damage potential, how easily it can be Reproduced, how much skill Exploitability requires, how many Affected users there are, and how easily the flaw can be Discovered - the other options are invented expansions that do not correspond to the model.$q$),
('ci-q-501','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',1,$q$In an attack tree, what does the root node represent?$q$,$q$["The attacker's ultimate goal","A single low-level exploit technique","The defender's mitigating control","The organization's overall risk appetite"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Attack trees are built top-down from the attacker's objective at the root (e.g., 'exfiltrate customer records'), with child nodes branching into the various ways that goal could be achieved; individual exploit techniques appear as leaf or intermediate nodes, not the root.$q$),
('ci-q-502','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',2,$q$An attacker sends emails that appear to come from the CFO's real address, convincing an employee to wire funds to a fraudulent account. Which STRIDE category does this attack primarily represent?$q$,$q$["Spoofing","Tampering","Denial of service","Elevation of privilege"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Impersonating another identity - here, forging the appearance of the CFO's email - is the definition of Spoofing in STRIDE, distinct from altering data (Tampering), disrupting availability (DoS), or gaining greater privilege (Elevation of privilege).$q$),
('ci-q-503','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',2,$q$A system administrator deletes audit log entries covering the exact window during which they made an unauthorized configuration change, later denying the change ever happened. Which STRIDE category best fits this action?$q$,$q$["Repudiation","Information disclosure","Spoofing","Denial of service"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Deleting or altering logs specifically to deny having performed an action is the hallmark of Repudiation in STRIDE - the threat is to accountability and non-repudiation, not to confidentiality (information disclosure), identity (spoofing), or availability (DoS).$q$),
('ci-q-504','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',2,$q$A low-privileged application user discovers an unvalidated parameter that lets them execute commands with administrator rights. Which STRIDE category does this represent?$q$,$q$["Elevation of privilege","Tampering","Repudiation","Spoofing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Gaining greater rights or capability than originally granted is Elevation of privilege in STRIDE - here the user moves from a low-privilege context to administrator-level execution, which is distinct from altering data, denying an action, or impersonating an identity.$q$),
('ci-q-505','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',2,$q$A well-funded group conducts a multi-year, low-noise intrusion campaign against a defense contractor, prioritizing long-term access to classified design data over any immediate financial payout. Which threat actor category best fits this description?$q$,$q$["A nation-state advanced persistent threat (APT)","A script kiddie","A hacktivist","An opportunistic cybercriminal"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sustained, well-resourced, stealthy operations aimed at strategic intelligence gathering rather than quick financial gain are the signature of a nation-state APT; script kiddies lack the skill for this, hacktivists favor visible disruption over stealth, and opportunistic cybercriminals chase fast financial returns.$q$),
('ci-q-506','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',1,$q$Which threat actor category is uniquely defined by already holding legitimate access or trust within the organization before acting maliciously?$q$,$q$["An insider threat","A hacktivist","A nation-state APT","A script kiddie"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An insider threat is defined by its starting position - legitimate employees, contractors, or partners who already hold authorized access - which sets it apart from all the external actor categories, whatever their skill or motivation.$q$),
('ci-q-507','cissp',1,'1.11','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are official STRIDE threat categories?$q$,$q$["Tampering","Elevation of privilege","Phishing","Ransomware"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Tampering and Elevation of privilege are two of STRIDE's six categories (alongside Spoofing, Repudiation, Information disclosure, and Denial of service). Phishing and ransomware are specific attack techniques or malware types, not STRIDE categories - they could map into one or more STRIDE categories depending on how they are carried out, but are not part of the mnemonic itself.$q$),
('ci-q-508','cissp',1,'1.11','ordering',3,$q$Order the seven stages of the PASTA threat modeling methodology, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Define objectives","Define the technical scope","Application decomposition","Threat analysis","Vulnerability and weakness analysis","Attack modeling","Risk and impact analysis"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]'::jsonb,$q$PASTA proceeds from business objectives, through defining technical scope and decomposing the application, into analyzing threats and vulnerabilities, then modeling how an attack would actually unfold, and finally quantifying risk and business impact - each stage builds on the outputs of the one before it, which is why PASTA is described as risk-centric rather than a simple checklist.$q$),
('ci-q-509','cissp',1,'1.11','matching',2,$q$Match each threat actor type to its typical primary motivation.$q$,$q${"left":["Nation-state APT","Hacktivist","Organized cybercrime group","Script kiddie"],"right":["Geopolitical or strategic espionage advantage","Ideological or political statement-making","Direct financial gain","Personal notoriety or thrill, with minimal skill"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$Motivation is the key differentiator among threat actor types: nation-states pursue strategic/espionage advantage, hacktivists pursue ideological causes, organized crime pursues profit, and script kiddies act largely for personal thrill or bragging rights using tools they didn't build themselves.$q$),
('ci-q-510','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',2,$q$A team begins its threat modeling effort by first cataloging the organization's most sensitive data and systems, then working outward to identify what could threaten them. Which threat modeling approach does this best describe?$q$,$q$["Asset-centric","Attacker-centric","Software-centric","Vulnerability-centric"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Starting from what needs protecting - the assets - and working outward to identify relevant threats is the defining trait of an asset-centric approach, as opposed to starting from attacker profiles (attacker-centric) or from the system's architecture and data flows (software-centric).$q$),
('ci-q-511','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',1,$q$At which point in a project's lifecycle does threat modeling deliver the greatest value at the lowest cost?$q$,$q$["During the design phase, before implementation begins","After the system is already in production","During end-user acceptance testing only","After the first security incident occurs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Threats identified during design can be addressed through architecture and control choices at minimal cost; the same class of flaw discovered after implementation or production deployment requires far more expensive rework, and waiting for an actual incident means the cost has already been paid in damage.$q$),
('ci-q-512','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',3,$q$Two different analysts independently score the same identified threat using DREAD and arrive at noticeably different severity totals. What does this most directly illustrate?$q$,$q$["DREAD scores rely on subjective judgment, so results can vary between evaluators","DREAD is a purely categorical model with no numeric scoring","STRIDE has replaced DREAD entirely in modern practice","This indicates a data entry error, since DREAD scores are mathematically fixed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Each DREAD criterion is rated by human judgment rather than measured objectively, so different reviewers can reasonably reach different scores for identical threats - a well-known limitation of the model, in contrast to STRIDE's simpler yes/no categorization, which does not attempt to produce a numeric ranking at all.$q$),
('ci-q-513','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',2,$q$How does an organization's attack surface differ from a specific attack vector?$q$,$q$["The attack surface is the total set of exposed entry points across the system, while an attack vector is the specific path or technique used in a single attack","They are two names for the exact same concept","The attack surface only refers to network ports, while an attack vector refers only to malware","An attack vector is always larger in scope than the attack surface"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Attack surface describes everything an attacker could potentially target - every exposed interface, service, and input - while an attack vector is the concrete route a particular attack actually takes through part of that surface, such as a phishing email or an unpatched VPN endpoint.$q$),
('ci-q-514','cissp',1,'1.11','mcq',3,$q$A public-facing web form has no rate limiting, allowing an attacker to submit an overwhelming volume of requests and exhaust server resources so legitimate users cannot load the page. Which STRIDE category does this best represent?$q$,$q$["Denial of service","Information disclosure","Tampering","Spoofing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Exhausting resources so that legitimate users lose access is the definition of Denial of service in STRIDE; it doesn't expose data (information disclosure), alter stored data (tampering), or impersonate an identity (spoofing).$q$),
-- 1.12 supply chain risk management (15)
('ci-q-515','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',1,$q$What is a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) primarily used for?$q$,$q$["Quickly determining whether a newly disclosed vulnerability affects a product by listing its components and versions","Encrypting source code repositories","Calculating a vendor's insurance premium","Replacing the need for code signing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An SBOM is an inventory of a software product's components, libraries, and their versions, letting an organization quickly check exposure when a vulnerability is disclosed in a specific component - it is not an encryption tool, a pricing mechanism, or a substitute for code signing.$q$),
('ci-q-516','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$During a hardware audit, a security team discovers that several network switches contain an additional, undocumented chip not present in the manufacturer's official design specifications. What risk does this most directly illustrate?$q$,$q$["A hardware supply chain integrity compromise, potentially introducing a covert backdoor","A software licensing violation","An SLA breach by the switch vendor","A misconfigured firewall rule"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An undocumented component added somewhere in the manufacturing or distribution process is a textbook hardware supply chain integrity compromise - it can implement a hidden backdoor or exfiltration channel that software-level security controls cannot detect, since the tampering happened at the physical layer.$q$),
('ci-q-517','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following is a core purpose of a Service Level Agreement (SLA) with a vendor?$q$,$q$["To define measurable performance commitments, such as uptime and response time, with penalties for missing them","To grant the vendor unrestricted access to internal systems","To waive the customer's right to terminate the contract","To replace the need for a separate security assessment"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An SLA's core purpose is setting objective, measurable performance targets and the consequences for missing them, giving both parties a shared basis for evaluating the relationship - it does not grant blanket system access, waive termination rights, or substitute for assessing the vendor's security posture.$q$),
('ci-q-518','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$Why do organizations negotiate a right-to-audit clause into vendor contracts rather than relying on the vendor to volunteer information about its controls?$q$,$q$["It makes ongoing verification of the vendor's security controls an enforceable contractual right rather than something the vendor grants at its own discretion","It transfers all legal liability for a breach to the vendor automatically","It eliminates the need for the vendor to sign an SLA","It is required before any vendor relationship can legally begin"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A right-to-audit clause converts the ability to verify a vendor's controls from a favor the vendor might extend into a binding contractual right the customer can invoke - it doesn't automatically shift legal liability, replace an SLA, or serve as a universal legal prerequisite to contracting.$q$),
('ci-q-519','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$A company's approved cloud storage vendor relies on a separate data center operator that the company has never assessed or contracted with directly. What category of risk does that data center operator represent to the company?$q$,$q$["Fourth-party risk","First-party risk","Residual risk from the company's own operations","Zero-day risk"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Risk originating from your vendor's own subcontractors and suppliers - entities you have no direct contract or assessment relationship with - is fourth-party risk, managed indirectly by requiring your vendor to flow down security obligations to its own supply chain.$q$),
('ci-q-520','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$A company assesses every new vendor with the same lengthy on-site security audit, regardless of whether the vendor handles regulated customer data or simply supplies office snacks. What supply chain risk management practice would most improve this approach?$q$,$q$["Vendor risk tiering, so assessment depth matches the vendor's actual criticality and data access","Eliminating all vendor assessments to save time","Requiring every vendor to sign an identical SLA","Outsourcing all vendor relationships to a single reseller"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Vendor risk tiering matches the depth and frequency of assessment to each vendor's actual risk - critical vendors handling sensitive data get rigorous, recurring scrutiny, while low-risk vendors get a lighter process - rather than applying one uniform (and often wasteful) standard to every relationship.$q$),
('ci-q-521','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',3,$q$Attackers compromise a software vendor's build pipeline and insert malicious code into an otherwise legitimate update, which is then automatically pushed to thousands of the vendor's customers who trust the signed update. What best characterizes this attack?$q$,$q$["A software supply chain attack, since the compromise occurred upstream at the trusted distribution source rather than at each victim directly","A denial-of-service attack against the vendor's website","A physical hardware tampering incident","An insider threat limited to a single customer organization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Compromising a trusted upstream distribution point - here, the vendor's build pipeline - so that malicious code rides along with a legitimate, trusted update to every downstream customer is the defining pattern of a software supply chain attack; it is not a DoS, physical tampering, or an incident confined to one customer.$q$),
('ci-q-522','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$A company depends on a small vendor's custom application but worries the vendor could go out of business, leaving the company unable to maintain critical software it doesn't own the source code for. Which contractual arrangement most directly addresses this risk?$q$,$q$["Source code escrow","A non-disclosure agreement (NDA)","A right-to-audit clause","An SLA uptime guarantee"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Source code escrow deposits the vendor's source code with a neutral third party, to be released to the customer if a defined trigger occurs, such as the vendor's insolvency - directly protecting continuity of a critical application. An NDA protects confidential information, a right-to-audit clause verifies controls, and an SLA addresses performance, none of which grant access to the code itself.$q$),
('ci-q-523','cissp',1,'1.12','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following belong in a thorough third-party vendor risk assessment?$q$,$q$["The vendor's financial stability and viability as an ongoing business","Evidence of the vendor's security certifications and independent audit reports","The vendor's marketing budget for the upcoming year","The physical color scheme of the vendor's office lobby"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Financial stability affects whether the vendor will remain a going concern able to honor its obligations, and independent security certifications/audit reports provide objective evidence of the vendor's control environment - both are core inputs to a risk assessment. Marketing budget and office decor have no bearing on the vendor's security or continuity risk.$q$),
('ci-q-524','cissp',1,'1.12','ordering',3,$q$Order the stages of the third-party vendor risk management lifecycle, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Identify the business need and candidate vendors","Perform due diligence and a security risk assessment","Negotiate the contract, including security requirements and SLAs","Onboard the vendor and provision access","Continuously monitor the vendor throughout the relationship","Offboard the vendor and revoke access at contract end"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3,4,5]'::jsonb,$q$The lifecycle begins with identifying why a vendor is needed, then assessing candidates before any contract exists, negotiating security terms into the contract, onboarding with appropriately scoped access, monitoring continuously for the life of the relationship (not just once), and finally formally offboarding to revoke access and reclaim or destroy data when the relationship ends.$q$),
('ci-q-525','cissp',1,'1.12','matching',2,$q$Match each supply chain risk management term to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["SBOM","Right-to-audit clause","Fourth-party risk","SLA"],"right":["An inventory of a software product's components and versions","A contract provision allowing inspection of a vendor's controls","Risk from a vendor's own subcontractors and suppliers","A contract defining measurable performance targets and penalties"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$Each term addresses a distinct piece of supply chain risk management: an SBOM inventories software components, a right-to-audit clause enables verification of controls, fourth-party risk covers your vendor's own supply chain, and an SLA sets measurable performance expectations with consequences for missing them.$q$),
('ci-q-526','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of code signing in the software supply chain?$q$,$q$["To let recipients verify the software's publisher and confirm it has not been altered since it was signed","To automatically encrypt all data the software later processes","To guarantee the software contains no vulnerabilities","To replace the need for a vendor security assessment"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Code signing provides cryptographic proof of the publisher's identity and that the code hasn't been modified post-signing, defending against tampered or impersonated updates - it says nothing about the presence of vulnerabilities and doesn't encrypt runtime data or substitute for assessing the vendor itself.$q$),
('ci-q-527','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',3,$q$A build system is configured to check a public package registry for a dependency before checking the organization's internal private registry. An attacker publishes a malicious package to the public registry using the exact same name as the organization's internal package. What attack does this scenario describe?$q$,$q$["A dependency confusion attack","A denial-of-service attack","A man-in-the-middle attack on network traffic","A brute-force credential attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Dependency confusion exploits build tooling that checks public registries before (or instead of) an organization's private registry, tricking it into pulling and executing an attacker-published package that shares the internal package's name - it is a supply chain trust exploit, not a DoS, network MITM, or credential attack.$q$),
('ci-q-528','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$What kind of guidance does NIST SP 800-161 provide?$q$,$q$["Practices for identifying, assessing, and mitigating cybersecurity risk throughout the information and communications technology supply chain","Requirements for classifying data by sensitivity level","Standards for physical fire suppression systems in data centers","Guidance exclusively for personal password complexity rules"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NIST SP 800-161 focuses specifically on cybersecurity supply chain risk management - vetting suppliers, verifying component provenance, and monitoring ongoing risk across the ICT supply chain - unrelated to data classification, fire suppression, or password policy, which are addressed by other standards.$q$),
('ci-q-529','cissp',1,'1.12','mcq',2,$q$A company's vendor contract requires the vendor to encrypt all customer data at rest, notify the company of any breach within 24 hours, and maintain a specific industry security certification. What is this contract language an example of?$q$,$q$["Minimum security requirements written into the contract","A right-to-audit clause","A non-compete agreement","A source code escrow arrangement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Naming specific, mandatory security controls the vendor must maintain as a condition of the contract - encryption, breach notification timelines, certifications - is exactly what minimum security requirements clauses do; a right-to-audit clause instead grants inspection rights, and the other two options address unrelated concerns.$q$),
-- 1.13 security awareness, education, and training (15)
('ci-q-530','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',1,$q$According to the NIST SP 800-50 continuum, what distinguishes 'awareness' from 'training'?$q$,$q$["Awareness delivers brief, repeated reminders of what to do and not do for everyone, while training builds a specific skill for a defined role or task","Awareness is mandatory while training is always optional","Training only applies to executives, while awareness applies to all other staff","There is no meaningful difference between the two terms"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NIST SP 800-50 treats awareness as broad, repeated reinforcement of basic dos-and-don'ts for the whole workforce, and training as building a concrete, applicable skill tied to a specific role or task - a narrower, deeper activity than awareness, not an optional/mandatory distinction or one limited to a particular audience.$q$),
('ci-q-531','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',2,$q$A company gives its software developers a course on secure coding and injection prevention, while giving its general office staff a course on recognizing phishing emails. What security training principle does this best illustrate?$q$,$q$["Role-based training, tailoring content to what each job function actually needs","Punitive training, penalizing employees for security failures","Compliance-only training, aimed solely at meeting a regulatory checkbox","Generic training, giving every employee identical content"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Matching training content to the specific risks and skills relevant to each job function - secure coding for developers, phishing recognition for general staff - is the definition of role-based training, as opposed to punitive, purely compliance-driven, or one-size-fits-all generic approaches.$q$),
('ci-q-532','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',2,$q$A security team notices that after each simulated phishing campaign, a growing percentage of employees use the 'report phishing' button rather than simply ignoring or deleting the suspicious email. What does a rising report rate most directly indicate?$q$,$q$["Employees are actively engaging in defending the organization, not just passively avoiding a click","The organization's email filtering has stopped working entirely","The simulated phishing emails have become too easy to identify","Employees are forwarding the emails to external parties"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A rising report rate shows employees are taking the additional, engaged step of flagging suspicious mail for the security team - letting the organization act quickly - rather than merely not clicking, which is a passive outcome that says less about genuine security engagement.$q$),
('ci-q-533','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',2,$q$Which trend, tracked over successive phishing simulation campaigns, best demonstrates that a security awareness program is producing an actual behavioral improvement rather than a one-time result?$q$,$q$["A sustained downward trend in click rate combined with a sustained upward trend in report rate across multiple campaigns over time","A single campaign in which zero employees clicked a very obvious lure","The total number of training slides included in that quarter's course","The number of employees who were sent a calendar invite for training"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A single easy campaign or a raw slide/invite count says little about real behavior change; a durable trend across multiple campaigns - clicks trending down and reports trending up together - is the kind of longitudinal evidence that indicates the program is genuinely shifting behavior rather than reflecting one easy test or administrative activity.$q$),
('ci-q-534','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',1,$q$What is the purpose of applying gamification, such as leaderboards or recognition badges, to a security awareness program?$q$,$q$["To increase engagement and encourage positive security behaviors through reward rather than punishment","To publicly shame employees who fail a phishing simulation","To replace the need for any measurable training metrics","To satisfy a specific legal mandate that all training include a game"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Gamification motivates participation and reinforces good behavior through positive recognition, which tends to sustain engagement better than punitive approaches - it doesn't eliminate the need to measure outcomes, nor is it a legal requirement.$q$),
('ci-q-535','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',1,$q$When is the most appropriate time to deliver an employee's first security awareness training?$q$,$q$["During onboarding, before or as system access is granted","Only during the organization's next scheduled annual refresher","Only after the employee has been involved in a security incident","Security training is not necessary for new employees during their first year"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$New hires are often specifically targeted because they are unfamiliar with organizational norms and contacts, so training delivered during onboarding - before or as access is granted - closes that early window of vulnerability rather than leaving it open until the next scheduled cycle or an actual incident occurs.$q$),
('ci-q-536','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',2,$q$Why do security awareness programs typically design separate, more targeted training content for executives and board members rather than reusing the general staff phishing course?$q$,$q$["Executives are high-value targets for well-researched, personalized attacks such as whaling and business email compromise","Executives are legally exempt from all standard security training requirements","Executives never use email, so phishing content is irrelevant to them","General staff training is too advanced for an executive audience"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Executives and board members hold financial authority and access to sensitive decisions that make them attractive targets for highly tailored attacks like whaling and business email compromise, so their training focuses on recognizing sophisticated, personalized social engineering rather than the broader, more generic content aimed at the general workforce.$q$),
('ci-q-537','cissp',1,'1.13','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are meaningful indicators of security awareness program effectiveness over time?$q$,$q$["A sustained increase in the phishing simulation report rate","An improving trend in post-training knowledge assessment scores","The total dollar amount spent on the training platform license","The number of unique font styles used in the training slides"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$A rising report rate reflects genuine engaged behavior, and improving knowledge assessment scores over time reflect retained understanding - both are outcome-oriented indicators. License cost and slide styling are administrative or cosmetic details unrelated to whether the program is actually changing behavior or knowledge.$q$),
('ci-q-538','cissp',1,'1.13','ordering',2,$q$Order the phases of a security awareness and training program lifecycle, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Design: assess needs and define goals and audiences","Develop: build the training content and materials","Implement: deliver the training to the workforce","Evaluate and maintain: measure effectiveness and update content"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$A training program logically starts by designing around identified needs and target audiences, then develops the actual materials, implements by delivering that content, and finally evaluates outcomes and maintains/updates the program - after which the cycle repeats as needs and threats change.$q$),
('ci-q-539','cissp',1,'1.13','matching',2,$q$Match each training audience to the content focus most appropriate for their role.$q$,$q${"left":["Executives and board members","Software developers","System administrators with privileged access","General office staff"],"right":["Recognizing targeted whaling and business email compromise attempts","Secure coding practices and injection prevention","Privileged-access hygiene and credential protection","Recognizing and reporting general phishing and social engineering"]}$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2,3]'::jsonb,$q$Role-based training matches content to what each audience actually needs to defend against in their job: executives face targeted whaling, developers need secure coding skills, privileged administrators need credential hygiene, and general staff need broad phishing/social engineering awareness.$q$),
('ci-q-540','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',2,$q$A company's employees technically complete 100% of their mandatory annual security training but continue to fall for basic phishing attempts at a high rate. What does this gap most likely indicate?$q$,$q$["The program is compliance-driven rather than having built a genuine security culture","The training platform's tracking system is broken","Employees are being trained too frequently","The company should discontinue phishing simulations entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$High completion paired with continued poor real-world behavior is a classic sign that training satisfied a compliance checkbox without producing genuine understanding or habit change - the fix is usually to improve content relevance and reinforcement (security culture), not to reduce simulations or assume a tracking glitch.$q$),
('ci-q-541','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',2,$q$Why might publicly naming and penalizing employees who fail a simulated phishing test undermine a security awareness program's long-term goals?$q$,$q$["It discourages honest reporting of real mistakes and suspicious activity out of fear of punishment","It is illegal in every jurisdiction to run phishing simulations at all","It guarantees a permanent reduction in click rates going forward","It has no measurable effect on employee behavior either way"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A punitive, shaming response teaches employees to hide mistakes rather than report them, which is the opposite of what the program needs when a real incident occurs; it doesn't guarantee lasting behavior change and simulations themselves are a widely used, legal practice - the risk is specifically in how failures are handled.$q$),
('ci-q-542','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',3,$q$A phishing simulation program has used the same obvious, poorly-written lure template for two years, and click rates have fallen close to zero. What should the program do next, and why?$q$,$q$["Introduce more sophisticated, realistic lure templates, because static difficulty no longer meaningfully tests real-world resilience","Discontinue phishing simulations entirely, since the goal has been achieved","Reduce the frequency of simulations to once every five years","Publish the results externally to demonstrate compliance to customers"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A near-zero click rate against a stale, easy template mostly shows employees have memorized that specific pattern, not that they would resist a genuinely sophisticated attack - escalating realism keeps the test calibrated to actual attacker capability rather than declaring victory prematurely or abandoning the exercise.$q$),
('ci-q-543','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',1,$q$An employee completes a mandatory security awareness quiz with a perfect score. What can be concluded from this result alone?$q$,$q$["That the employee could recall the tested material at that moment, but not necessarily that their real-world behavior has changed","That the employee will never fall for a phishing attempt","That the entire organization's security culture is now mature","That no further training or reinforcement is needed for that employee"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A quiz score reflects short-term recall under test conditions, not proven behavior under real pressure - a single perfect score cannot guarantee future resistance to phishing, doesn't speak to organizational culture as a whole, and doesn't eliminate the value of ongoing reinforcement.$q$),
('ci-q-544','cissp',1,'1.13','mcq',3,$q$Over an 18-month period, an organization tracks a steady decline in security incidents attributable to human error alongside a steady rise in the phishing simulation report rate. Compared to relying on quarterly training-quiz average scores alone, why is this combination considered stronger evidence of program effectiveness?$q$,$q$["It reflects sustained, real-world behavior change over time rather than short-term recall measured in an artificial test setting","Quiz scores are entirely unrelated to security awareness and should never be tracked","Incident counts are always a perfectly precise measurement with no other contributing factors","Report rate is a legally mandated metric while incident tracking is optional"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Longitudinal, real-world indicators - fewer human-error incidents and more proactive reporting sustained over many months - demonstrate that behavior actually changed under real conditions, which is stronger evidence than short-term quiz recall, even though quiz scores still have some value and incident counts can have other contributing factors.$q$);
